      <tei:div type="colophon" subtype="notes">
        <tei:p>“Adon Olam” is a piyyut that became popular in the 15th century and is often attributed to Solomon ibn Gabirol (1021–1058) and less often to Sherira Gaon (900-1001), or his son, Hai ben Sherira Gaon (939-1038). The variation of the piyyut appearing here is the 10 line (5 stanza) version familiar to Ashkenazi congregations. (Sefaradi siddurim have 12 line (six stanza) variants, and there are some with 14 or 15 lines.)</tei:p>
        <tei:p>The German translation of “Adon Olam” appearing here is as found in Rabbi David Einhorn’s עלת תמיד <tei:hi rend="italic">Gebetbuch für Israelitische Reform-Gemeinden</tei:hi> (1858), pp. 1-2. The English translation here, by Joshua Giorgio-Rubin, translating Rabbi David Einhorn, is as found in Rubin’s <tei:hi rend="italic">Olat Hadashah: A Modern Adaptation of David Einhorn’s Olat Tamid for Shabbat Evening</tei:hi> (2020), p. 14.</tei:p>
      </tei:div>
      <j:endConditional target="#osp_46032_notes"/>
